Documentation: gg.core.ApiError
----
struct ApiError { let code: core.ApiErrorCode; let operation: String; let message: String; var description: String { get } }

Defined in `gg.core`, brought into scope with `import gg`.

A gg call that failed.

Every fallible function in this SDK is `throws` and throws one of these, which is what a Swift author expects of a fallible call and what makes `try` the whole of the ceremony: `let skill = try skills.readSkill("testing")` binds a `String`, and a failure ends the program with gg told exactly which call failed and on which line.

A failure a program expects is an ordinary `catch` on `code`, which is a value rather than prose:

```swift
do {
    try views.openFile("notes.md")
} catch let failure as core.ApiError where failure.code == .notFound {
    try files.writeFile("notes.md", contents: "")
}
```
  code: core.ApiErrorCode — The failure class, so a catch site branches on a value rather than on prose.
  operation: String — The gg call that failed, under gg's own name for it (`read_file`, `spawn_subagent`).
  message: String — What went wrong, in gg's words. Worth showing in a view; not worth matching on.
  description: String — gg's own sentence about a failed call: the call, the class, and what went wrong.

It is gg's convention rather than Swift's. The same line is what the ECMAScript guest's shim writes and what the native tool-calling path shows, and it is what a model reads when a failure escapes — so two arms whose uncaught failures read differently would be two arms whose error rates a study could not compare.

